# The default policy for a Toolbox host.
#
# A host registers every subsystem it runs and describes each one from the
# contract that subsystem serves. What an agent may then call is not a property of
# that registration: it is this document, and a deployment that wants a different
# answer supplies a different one.
#
# This default grants every read in the deployment and nothing that changes state.
# It is deliberately the narrower half. Granting writes means naming the
# deployments and actors that may change something, which is a decision somebody
# has to make rather than a default somebody has to accept.
#
# A policy is a list of rules. Each line is a decision, a pattern over operation
# identifiers, and optionally the classes of operation it applies to. Patterns are
# matched most specific first and the first match decides, so a narrow rule can
# carve an exception out of a broad one:
#
#   allow knowledge/**      read write
#   deny  registry/**       write
#
# "*" matches one segment of an identifier and a trailing "**" matches the rest,
# so "knowledge/**" is every operation of the knowledge API. An identifier is
# "<api>/<service>/<method>": the API is part of it because three subsystems serve
# the same contract, and naming one provider has to be possible.
#
# The classes are read, write, and unclassified. An operation whose contract
# declared nothing is unclassified, and it is not a read: a rule that names no
# class is a statement about every operation, which is how a deployment
# deliberately trusts one.

allow *  read
